The original uploaded artifacts remain archived rather than being silently discarded.

pom.xml

The first implementation should establish Java 21 and the Jakarta Mail API/Angus provider boundary.

<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="
           http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
           https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <groupId>app.javamail</groupId>
    <artifactId>javamail.app</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <name>javamail.app</name>
    <description>
        Modern Jakarta Mail application infrastructure for SMTP, IMAP,
        POP3, MIME, synchronization and secure mail processing.
    </description>
    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <jakarta.mail.version>2.1.5</jakarta.mail.version>
        <angus.mail.version>2.0.5</angus.mail.version>
        <junit.version>5.11.0</junit.version>
    </properties>
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>jakarta.mail</groupId>
            <artifactId>jakarta.mail-api</artifactId>
            <version>${jakarta.mail.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.eclipse.angus</groupId>
            <artifactId>angus-mail</artifactId>
            <version>${angus.mail.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>${junit.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.13.0</version>
                <configuration>
                    <release>${java.version}</release>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.0</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>

The jakarta.mail-api artifact is the API boundary; Angus supplies the implementation/provider layer. Maven Central documents the Angus provider as org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail. 

Core package

app.javamail

The first executable class:

package app.javamail;
public final class Application {
    private Application() {
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("javamail.app v1");
    }
}

That intentionally starts extremely small.

The first milestone is not a giant framework. It is:

compile
  ↓
start
  ↓
create mail session
  ↓
connect
  ↓
send/receive

Configuration

MailConfig.java:

package app.javamail.config;
public record MailConfig(
        String smtpHost,
        int smtpPort,
        boolean smtpStartTls,
        String imapHost,
        int imapPort,
        boolean imapStartTls,
        String username
) {
}

AppConfig.java:

package app.javamail.config;
public record AppConfig(
        MailConfig mail
) {
    public static AppConfig defaults() {
        return new AppConfig(
                new MailConfig(
                        "localhost",
                        587,
                        true,
                        "localhost",
                        143,
                        true,
                        ""
                )
        );
    }
}

This gives us a configuration abstraction before introducing environment-variable parsing, secrets managers, or database-backed accounts.

Domain model

MessageAddress.java:

package app.javamail.domain.message;
public record MessageAddress(
        String name,
        String email
) {
}

MessageId.java:

package app.javamail.domain.message;
public record MessageId(String value) {
    public MessageId {
        if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Message ID cannot be blank");
        }
    }
}

MailMessage.java:

package app.javamail.domain.message;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.List;
public record MailMessage(
        MessageId id,
        String subject,
        List<MessageAddress> from,
        List<MessageAddress> to,
        List<MessageAddress> cc,
        String textBody,
        String htmlBody,
        List<MessageAttachment> attachments,
        Instant sentAt,
        Instant receivedAt,
        String inReplyTo,
        List<String> references
) {
}

MessageAttachment.java:

package app.javamail.domain.message;
public record MessageAttachment(
        String id,
        String filename,
        String contentType,
        long size,
        String sha256,
        boolean inline
) {
}

This is deliberately independent of jakarta.mail.Message.

That distinction will matter later when we add storage, Web4 semantics, and LMLM processing.

Mail provider boundary

package app.javamail.mail;
