SMTP service

The application-facing interface:

package app.javamail.mail.smtp;
import app.javamail.domain.message.MailMessage;
public interface SmtpClient {
    void send(MailMessage message);
}

Implementation:

package app.javamail.mail.smtp;
import jakarta.mail.Session;
import jakarta.mail.Transport;
import jakarta.mail.internet.InternetAddress;
import jakarta.mail.internet.MimeMessage;
import app.javamail.domain.message.MailMessage;
public final class AngusSmtpClient implements SmtpClient {
    private final Session session;
    public AngusSmtpClient(Session session) {
        this.session = session;
    }
    @Override
    public void send(MailMessage message) {
        try {
            MimeMessage mime = new MimeMessage(session);
            mime.setSubject(message.subject(), "UTF-8");
            if (!message.from().isEmpty()) {
                var sender = message.from().getFirst();
                mime.setFrom(
                        new InternetAddress(
                                sender.email(),
                                sender.name(),
                                "UTF-8"
                        )
                );
            }
            for (var recipient : message.to()) {
                mime.addRecipient(
                        jakarta.mail.Message.RecipientType.TO,
                        new InternetAddress(
                                recipient.email(),
                                recipient.name(),
                                "UTF-8"
                        )
                );
            }
            mime.setText(
                    message.textBody() == null
                            ? ""
                            : message.textBody(),
                    "UTF-8"
            );
            Transport.send(mime);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new MailSendException(
                    "Unable to send message",
                    e
            );
        }
    }
}

Exception:

package app.javamail.mail.smtp;
public final class MailSendException
        extends RuntimeException {
    public MailSendException(
            String message,
            Throwable cause
    ) {
        super(message, cause);
    }
}

Now we have an actual mail path:

MailMessage
     ↓
SmtpClient
     ↓
MimeMessage
     ↓
Transport
     ↓
SMTP

IMAP boundary

Next:

package app.javamail.mail.imap;
public interface ImapClient {
    void connect();
    void disconnect();
    void synchronize();
}

Then the Angus implementation will own the Jakarta Mail Store and Folder objects.

That keeps this:

Store
Folder
Message
FetchProfile
SearchTerm

out of the rest of the application.

MIME boundary

package app.javamail.mail.mime;
import app.javamail.domain.message.MailMessage;
public interface MimeParser {
    MailMessage parse(
            jakarta.mail.Message message
    );
}

And:

package app.javamail.mail.mime;
import app.javamail.domain.message.MailMessage;
public interface MimeBuilder {
    jakarta.mail.internet.MimeMessage build(
            jakarta.mail.Session session,
            MailMessage message
    );
}

This creates the clean boundary:

Application Model
       ↕
MIME Adapter
       ↕
Jakarta Mail

Security boundary

package app.javamail.security;
public record TlsPolicy(
        boolean required,
        boolean verifyCertificate
) {
}

And:

package app.javamail.security;
public interface SecurityPolicy {
    TlsPolicy tls();
    long maximumMessageSize();
    long maximumAttachmentSize();
    boolean allowHtml();
    boolean allowRemoteResources();
}

The important V1 rule

No application code should look like this:

Session session = Session.getInstance(...);
Store store = session.getStore(...);
Folder inbox = store.getFolder("INBOX");

except inside the mail adapter layer.

Instead:

Application
   ↓
MailService
   ↓
ImapClient
   ↓
Angus
   ↓
Jakarta Mail

That one decision will save us a lot of architectural cleanup later.

Repository migration

The existing ZIP should therefore be transformed like this:

OLD
─────────────────────
README.md
empty protocol/
empty models/
empty headers/
old Info.plist
mail artifacts

into:

NEW
─────────────────────
README.md
pom.xml
src/
docs/
tests
Docker
GitHub Actions
mail engine
security
storage
sync
API

while preserving the old snapshot:

docs/archive/original-repository/

First executable milestone

The first working release target should be:

javamail.app 0.1.0

with exactly four verified capabilities:

1. Build
2. Create authenticated mail session
3. Send SMTP message
4. Connect to IMAP and enumerate mailbox

Then:

0.2.0 → MIME + attachments
0.3.0 → IMAP synchronization
0.4.0 → storage
0.5.0 → REST API
0.6.0 → security hardening
0.7.0 → OAuth2/OpenPGP
0.8.0 → observability
0.9.0 → deployment/CI
1.0.0 → production baseline

After that, the next concrete step is to generate the complete repository files, not just snippets: pom.xml, all Java source files, tests, configuration, Docker, Compose, GitHub Actions, documentation, and the archive migration manifest. That will give us an actual buildable javamail.app v1 rather than another specification.
